outcome: {{ resolution.actual_outcome }}
{% if resolution.surprises %}surprises: {{ resolution.surprises | join(' · ') }}
{% endif %}{% if resolution.notes %}notes: {{ resolution.notes }}{% endif %}

{% if verdicts %}
HOW EACH MODULE FARED
{% for verdict in verdicts %}- {{ verdict.module }}: {{ verdict.verdict }}{% if verdict.justification %} — {{ verdict.justification }}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

WHAT EACH MODULE REMEMBERS
{% for module, kind in kinds.items() %}
{{ module }} → kind `{{ kind }}`: {{ rules[kind] }}
{% endfor %}

RULES

- A memory must be a **durable fact about this user or their world**, useful in a
  future decision that is not this one. "They chose to stay" is a fact about this
  decision and belongs on the card, not in memory. "Their manager makes commitments
  verbally and does not put them in writing" is durable, and belongs here.
- Write each one so it still makes sense read cold in six months, with no reference
  to "this decision" or "the council".
- Never invent a name. Use the role label the user used.
- `salience` 0–1: how likely this is to matter again. Be strict — everything marked
  important means nothing is.
- At most three memories per module. Most decisions produce one or two in total.
- If nothing durable was learned, return an empty list. That is the common case and
  is not a failure.
